Canada has always had a reputation as  being the well-behaved British colony  whilst its southern neighbor rebelled  and broke free the British Northern  Territory stayed loyal and wanted  nothing to do with it yet Canada like so  many other territories of the British  Empire did have its own armed revolution  which will be a surprise to many so  Canada didn't really exist at this point  and was actually a series of British  colonies the two important ones were  Upper Canada capital Toronto and to its  north Lower Canada focused on Montreal  and being host to a majority  french-speaking population throughout  the early nineteenth century the people  of these two canada's had wished for  wide sweeping reforms to the colonies  and the answer in case you were  wondering was no this was because both  of the Canada's were run by closed-off  anti-democratic oligarchy collectives in  Lower Canada it was the Chateau clique  made up of mostly Englishmen who wanted  the French speakers there to integrate  to an English way of life which in case  you're wondering did not endear them to  the population in Upper Canada it was  the family compact in charge and it was  also hostile to democracy these groups  obviously had their critics in Upper  Canada the primary critic was William  Lyon Mackenzie who led the reform  movement and in Lower Canada it was  louise joseph papp anew the leader of a  group called the Patriots in 1832 after  some protesters in Montreal were shot by  Crown soldiers Papineau sent a list of  demands to the British government in  order to reform the colony Britain and  the lower Canadian leaders decided that  they weren't going to launch any reforms  and it actually everything was perfect  already and over the next five years  tensions continued to rise in the  deadlock continued in November 1837  things started to turn a bit radical and  the lower Canadian government attempted  to arrest Pappa knew he fled south to  the United States and his supporters  began to violently resist government  attempts to round people up the  government wasn't able to suppress this  quickly and it soon turned into a  full-scale revolt against British rule  in Upper Canada seeing their neighbors  revolt they were in full swing Mackenzie  and his followers followed suit and  began their own uprising they did well  at first because the troops there had  been moved out of the province but the  revolutionaries had one major issue  they weren't professional soldiers this  didn't stop Mackenzie from declaring the  creation of the Republic of Canada which  then promptly collapsed that the  revolutionaries were defeated by local  militiamen you would assume that that  was it them for the Canadian revolution  but fun fact  no the rebellion had garnered a great  deal of interest in the United States  and many there were sympathetic to the  Canadians plight some Americans formed  groups which welcome Mackenzie and Pappa  knew and sought to help them in making  Canada Republic this eventually led to a  group of American volunteers could the  hunter brothers crossing the border in  invading Canada and it wasn't long  before British troops killed Americans  which inflamed tensions  now obviously armed American citizens  invading Canada was a cause for concern  in both London and Washington who didn't  want war in the end both sides came to  an agreement to suppress Americans  crossing the border and in Canada itself  the lower Canadian rebellion had been  completely crushed by the end of 1838 so  what did this mean for Canada and the  rebels or Britain wanted to prevent a  rebellion from happening again and so it  tasked all durham to make a list of  recommendations for reform the first and  the only one that was initially followed  was the unification of the Canada's into  just one Canada and as for the leaders  of the rebellion they remained in exile  in the USA eventually though they were  pardoned and returned to Canada and so  the Canadian Revolution was over and  whilst it didn't create an independent  Canadian Republic it did to a certain  extent create Canada I hope you enjoyed  this episode and thank you for watching  with the special thanks to my patreon  supporters who you can see on screen  here and with an extra special thanks to  James Bissonnette David archaeologist  party-boy Co Danny Maloney Colin  Castleman Rob Waterhouse are in the  white Michael Reynolds H way an emperor  Chris wicker Gustav Swan Gareth Turner  spinning three plates David Silverman  Winston Cawood Maggie packs Kowski  Christian check' Anthony Beckett  Robert Wetzel Skye Chappell and Ike
 

